<p>A move in and move out inspection for a Tennessee rental should document the condition of every room, surface, fixture, and appliance with dated photos and a written checklist, signed by the tenant at move in, and repeated in the same order with the same angles at move out. That pair of records is what lets a landlord show the difference between normal wear and tear and actual damage, which is the line Tennessee's landlord tenant rules use for security deposit deductions. Without the move in record, the move out record proves very little, because there is nothing to compare it against.</p>

<p>This guide covers what Tennessee law expects around deposits, what a complete inspection documents, how to photograph it so the record holds up, and how the same inspection doubles as the scope for your turnover.</p>

<h2>What Tennessee law expects</h2>

<p>Tennessee's Uniform Residential Landlord and Tenant Act sets the rules for security deposits in the counties it covers, which include the more populous counties that make up much of Middle Tennessee. In general terms, a landlord may keep part of a deposit for unpaid rent and for damage beyond normal wear and tear, and has to give the tenant an itemized list of the damage and the cost of the repairs. Tenants have a right to see that list and to dispute it. The statute also sets deadlines for returning the deposit and requirements for how deposits are held.</p>

<p>The exact steps, timing, and wording matter, and they can depend on your county and your lease. Check the current statute, or have an attorney review your move out process once, so every deduction you take is one you can defend. This article covers the documentation side, which is the part a maintenance company sees every day.</p>

<h2>Why the move in inspection matters more than the move out</h2>

<p>Owners and managers tend to put their effort into the move out walk, because that is when money is on the line. But a move out photo of a stained carpet only proves the carpet is stained. It does not prove the stain is new. The move in record is the other half of the evidence, and it is the half most often missing or incomplete.</p>

<p>A strong move in inspection has three parts:</p>

<ul>
<li>A written checklist, room by room, with the condition of each item noted.</li>
<li>Photos of every room and every item on the checklist.</li>
<li>The tenant's signature, with a few days allowed for them to add anything they find after moving in.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What a complete inspection covers</h2>

<h3>Every room</h3>

<ul>
<li>Walls, including nail holes, scuffs, patches, and paint condition.</li>
<li>Ceilings, including any stains, cracks, or texture damage.</li>
<li>Floors, by type, with any stains, scratches, burns, tears, or loose transitions noted.</li>
<li>Doors, door hardware, door stops, and closet doors and tracks.</li>
<li>Windows, screens, blinds, and window locks.</li>
<li>Light fixtures, switches, outlets, and cover plates.</li>
<li>Smoke and carbon monoxide alarms, present and working.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Kitchen</h3>

<ul>
<li>Every appliance, inside and out, with make and model noted and a photo of the interior of the oven, refrigerator, and dishwasher.</li>
<li>Cabinets and drawers, including hinges, fronts, and the cabinet floor under the sink.</li>
<li>Countertops, backsplash, sink, faucet, and disposal.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Bathrooms</h3>

<ul>
<li>Toilet, including a quick check that it does not run.</li>
<li>Tub or shower, surround, caulk, and grout.</li>
<li>Vanity, faucet, mirror, and exhaust fan.</li>
<li>Any signs of leaks under the sink or around the toilet base.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Systems and exterior</h3>

<ul>
<li>HVAC working in both heat and cool, with the filter size noted and a photo of the filter condition.</li>
<li>Water heater, with no visible leaks.</li>
<li>Yard, fences, gates, decks, and any outbuildings if the tenant has access to them.</li>
<li>Garage door and opener, and the number of remotes handed over.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Things people forget</h3>

<ul>
<li>Meter readings on the day of move in and move out, if utilities transfer.</li>
<li>Number of keys, fobs, and remotes given and returned.</li>
<li>Light bulbs, all working or not.</li>
<li>Any items left in the unit for the tenant's use, such as a washer and dryer or window units.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to photograph it so the record holds up</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Wide then close.</strong> Take one wide photo of each wall from the doorway, then close ups of anything worth noting. The wide shot proves where the close up was taken.</li>
<li><strong>Same order, same angles.</strong> Walk the unit in the same order at move in and move out, standing in the same spots. Side by side comparisons are what make a deduction easy to understand.</li>
<li><strong>Dated files.</strong> Keep the original files with their date information intact, and store them with the lease, not on someone's phone.</li>
<li><strong>Video as a backup.</strong> A slow walkthrough video is a good supplement. It does not replace stills of specific items.</li>
<li><strong>Good light.</strong> Turn on every light and open the blinds. A dark photo of a carpet proves nothing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Drawing the line at move out</h2>

<p>The move out inspection is where wear and tear meets damage. Faded paint, light scuffs, small nail holes, and carpet worn in the walking path from years of use are generally treated as normal wear. Holes in walls, pet damage, burns, broken fixtures, and stains that were not there at move in generally are not. The age of the item matters too, because an eight year old carpet has less value left to charge for than a new one. Our article on <a href="/articles/wear-and-tear-vs-damage-tennessee/">wear and tear versus damage in Tennessee</a> covers that line in more detail, with examples.</p>

<p>Whatever you deduct, itemize it with the cost of the actual repair, attach the before and after photos, and keep the invoice. A deduction backed by a matching pair of photos and a real invoice is a deduction that rarely gets disputed.</p>

<h2>The move out inspection is also your turnover scope</h2>

<p>The same walk that documents damage for the deposit also tells you what the unit needs before the next tenant moves in. Every item on the move out checklist that is not in rent ready condition is a line in the turn scope: patch and paint here, replace blinds there, deep clean the oven, service the HVAC, replace the flapper in the hall bath.</p>

<p>Owners and managers who keep that list in the same format every time can get a turnover quoted and scheduled before the tenant even turns in the keys. That shortens vacancy more than any other single habit. Our <a href="/articles/rental-make-ready-checklist/">rental make ready checklist</a> lays out the order the work should happen in once the scope is set.</p>

<h2>Getting it done across a portfolio</h2>

<p>For a single rental, an owner can walk the unit with a phone and a checklist. For a property manager with dozens or hundreds of units, consistency is the hard part. Different coordinators photograph different things, records end up scattered, and a dispute six months later turns into a search for photos that may not exist.</p>

